  const CATEGORIES = [{"key": "summit", "label": "The grand tables — the Riva, a counter by the harbour, and the sea at Firule", "lead": "A terrace behind an unmarked door on the Riva, a twelve-seat counter by the harbour, and the sea terrace at Firule.", "story": {"title": "Terraces, a counter and the open sea", "story": "Split's grand tables split between the palace, the harbour and the sea. ZOI is built into the palace's south façade with a top-floor terrace over the Riva; Gault&Millau scores it 17/20 in 2026 and MICHELIN has recommended it since it opened in 2017. Krug, on the harbour front, is a counter of about twelve seats round an open kitchen, and in the 2025 guide it became the first table in the city to hold a MICHELIN star. Dvor, at Firule, a short walk east, faces the open channel from a garden terrace and scores 16/20. None publishes a winter schedule, and Dvor has historically closed for one: call before you promise any of them after October.", "where": "ZOI, Riva; Krug, Obala Ante Trumbića; Dvor, Firule"}}, {"key": "varos", "label": "Veli Varoš — the konobas of the fishermen's quarter", "lead": "Three konobas in the stone lanes of the fishermen's quarter, a few minutes west of the palace.", "story": {"title": "Grills of the fishermen's quarter", "story": "Veli Varoš, the old quarter of fishermen and farmers below Marjan, cooks the catch with little fuss: fish is shown raw, priced by weight and grilled. Konoba Fetivi on Tomića stine is the one with a 2026 MICHELIN listing, and Gault&Millau praises its black cuttlefish risotto. Its neighbour, Konoba Matejuška, is a small family grill named for the fishermen's harbour below, known for tuna marinated in red wine and herbs. Šperun, on the short street of the same name at the quarter's edge, grills sardines and sea bass until late. Only Fetivi has a guide rating; the other two are neighbourhood tables, so call ahead.", "where": "Konoba Fetivi, Veli Varoš; Konoba Matejuška, Matejuška; Šperun Eat and Drink, Veli Varoš"}}, {"key": "palace", "label": "In and around the palace walls", "lead": "An upstairs room in a Gothic palace, a counter that closes when the food runs out, and a wine-lined bistro by the fish hall.", "story": {"title": "Kitchens in and around the walls", "story": "People still live inside Diocletian's walls, and the kitchens in and around them range from a market counter to a Gothic townhouse. Villa Spiza, on Kružićeva, is a box of a room where Ivana Gamulin cooks what she bought that morning, takes cash only and no bookings. Apetit occupies an upper floor of the 15th-century Papalić Palace and keeps the old dishes, pašticada and even tripe. Bokeria, just outside the west wall beside the fish market, is the modern wine bistro of the three, glass-fronted and lined with bottles, at 13.5/20 with Gault&Millau. Villa Spiza works winter hours; the others publish none.", "where": "Villa Spiza, Palace; Apetit, Papalić Palace; Bokeria Kitchen & Wine, fish market"}}, {"key": "bistro", "label": "The new Split bistros — the wine list first", "lead": "A MICHELIN-listed wine bistro named for the Kaštela vines, and a small stone room of braised pot dishes.", "story": {"title": "The wine list first", "where": "Zinfandel Food & Wine Bistro, Centre; Šug, Tolstojeva", "story": "Split's newer tables start from the glass. Zinfandel, on Marulićeva, takes its name from the Kaštela vines whose story is told above, pours about fifteen Croatian wines by the glass and is in the MICHELIN Guide Croatia 2026. Šug, a small stone room on Tolstojeva by the palace's north-east corner, cooks braised pot dishes and held a MICHELIN Bib Gourmand from 2022; Gault&Millau scores it 13.5/20 in 2026. Both are small and both publish hours that disagree with one another's listings, so book at night and call in winter."}}, {"key": "locals", "label": "Where the city eats — a family konoba and the ćevapi", "lead": "A family konoba at the foot of the hill, and the ćevapi window by Marmontova.", "story": {"title": "Where the city eats", "where": "Konoba Marjan, Veli Varoš; Kantun Paulina, Matošića", "story": "Split's everyday tables cook from the morning fish market and the charcoal, not from a tasting menu. Konoba Marjan, a few tables on Senjska at the foot of the hill, is run by a father, mother and son who change the menu daily with the market, so the cuttlefish brujet or black risotto can be gone by half past seven. Kantun Paulina, on Matošića near Marmontova, has grilled ćevapi for decades and serves them the Split way, in a soft lepinja with ajvar and raw onion, by small, medium or large, cash only. Neither publishes a winter schedule: call or look in."}}, {"key": "street", "label": "La Rue — the two markets and the fishermen's buffet", "lead": "The green market at the Silver Gate, the fish hall, and the fishermen's buffet at Matejuška.", "story": {"title": "Markets and the fishermen's buffet", "story": "Split eats on its feet in the morning. The Pazar green market trades daily against the east wall of Diocletian's Palace by the Silver Gate, where family farms sell figs, greens, olive oil and Pag cheese from about six until early afternoon. A few minutes west, the Ribarnica off Marmontova sells the night's catch by weight, in cash, until about one. And at Matejuška, Buffet Fife has fed the waterfront for decades with pašticada and gnocchi, fish soup and fried squid at prices the locals still pay; it reportedly works all year. None of the three takes a booking.", "where": "Pazar, Silver Gate; Ribarnica, Marmontova; Buffet Fife, Matejuška"}}];
